This CSS fragment styles HTML with class="card", an image inside it, and class="badge". Put it in that page's CSS editor.

```css
* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
.card {
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 360px;
  margin: 18px auto;
  padding: 14px;
  border: 2px solid #456;
}
.card img {
  display: block;
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}
.badge {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: 3px 7px;
  background: #e1f3e9;
  border-radius: 6px;
}
```

The * selector matches elements generally. .card chooses a class. .card img chooses images anywhere inside a card. auto side margins center a capped card when extra width is available.

after:
This rule gives the card a width limit and centers it when there is spare space.

```css
.card {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 360px;
  margin: 18px auto;
  padding: 14px;
  border: 2px solid #456;
}
```

`.card` chooses parts with the card class. `360px` is the width limit. `auto` shares spare space between the left and right margins. The 18-pixel margins go above and below.

Keep the image no wider than the card’s content area.

```css
.card img {
  display: block;
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}
```

`.card img` chooses images inside the card. `height: auto` keeps the image’s width and height in their original proportion. `display: block` puts the image in the page’s block layout rather than on a line of text.

A badge is a small label beside other content.

```css
.badge {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: 3px 7px;
  border-radius: 6px;
}
```

`inline-block` lets the badge sit with text and still have box dimensions. Padding leaves space around its words. `border-radius` rounds its corners.
